Mechanical Low Back Pain
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Lumbosacral ache exacerbated by movement, Relief with rest, Absence of radiculopathy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-030
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe lower back spasm causing sudden immobility and an inability to stand upright or walk.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient who experienced a sudden, severe "catch" in the lower back while lifting, resulting in agonizing pain and an inability to stand. Urgent physical assessment was necessary to definitively exclude acute disc herniation with cauda equina syndrome or an acute vertebral compression fracture, and to provide immediate, potent muscle relaxants and analgesia to break the severe mechanical spasm cycle prior to daytime services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I bent over to pick up a laundry basket, felt a sudden grab in my lower back, and now the muscle spasms are so intense I can't stand up straight."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute onset of severe mechanical low back pain. Timeline: Patient was asymptomatic until 4 hours ago. While performing a loaded flexion movement (lifting laundry), experienced a sudden, sharp exacerbation or "catch" of pain in the lumbosacral region. Rapidly progressed over the next hour into intense, deep, aching pain accompanied by severe muscle spasms. Pain is strictly localized to the lumbar spine and does not extend past the gluteal folds. Pain is predominantly exacerbated by any truncal movement, specifically attempting to stand fully upright. Patient achieves significant relief of pain only by resting in a supine position with hips and knees flexed over a pillow. Denies bowel or bladder incontinence, numbness in the saddle area, or leg weakness.
Functional Status: Temporarily bedbound; mobilizing to the bathroom requires crawling or severe stooping.
Social/Safety Concerns: Fall risk due to severe pain-induced yielding of the paraspinal muscles.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/88 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 88 Kg
General: Alert, in visible distress, lying supine with knees bent, actively resisting any requests to move the torso.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination is completely normal: 5/5 motor strength in both lower extremities, intact dermatomal sensation to light touch/pinprick, and symmetrical 2+ patellar and Achilles reflexes. Plantar responses are downgoing. Negative straight leg raise test bilaterally (no radicular pain radiating below the knee). Inspection of the back reveals prominent, symmetrical paraspinal muscle spasms visible and palpable as taut, tender bands in the lumbar region. Restricted active lumbar range of motion, most notably an absolute inability to perform forward flexion or extension due to mechanical pain blocking. No focal midline bony point tenderness.
Environmental Observations: Laundry basket left exactly where the injury occurred.
Medication Reconciliation: Took Ibuprofen 400mg 2 hours ago with zero relief of the severe muscle spasm.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Mechanical Low Back Pain (Lumbar Strain/Spasm) - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Cauda Equina Syndrome - Ruled out definitively by the completely intact lower limb neurology, normal reflexes, negative straight leg raise, and absolute absence of bowel/bladder or saddle sensory dysfunction.

Lumbar Radiculopathy (Sciatica) - Ruled out by the absence of pain radiating down the leg past the knee and the negative straight leg raise.

Vertebral Compression Fracture - Ruled out clinically by the mechanism of injury (muscular lifting rather than a high-impact fall or underlying severe osteoporosis) and the lack of focal, exquisite midline bony tenderness.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a classic, severe acute lumbar strain characterized by sudden-onset paraspinal spasm following a loaded flexion mechanism. The critical clinical imperative was to rule out emergent neurological red flags. A rigorous examination confirmed the absolute absence of radiculopathy or cauda equina syndrome, firmly establishing the pathology as localized, intense mechanical muscular failure. The severity of the spasms rendered the patient functionally paralyzed and in extreme discomfort. Delaying intervention would have left the patient in an unsafe, immobilized state. Urgent after-hours assessment safely provided diagnostic reassurance, excluding catastrophic spinal injury, and allowed for the immediate administration of targeted muscle relaxants to break the debilitating spasm cycle.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Intramuscular Ketorolac 30mg STAT. Prescribed oral Diazepam 2mg BD PRN for 3 days to explicitly target the paraspinal hypertonicity.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Lie flat on the floor with your calves resting on the seat of a chair (hips and knees at 90 degrees) for 15 minutes. Consequence: Complete mechanical unloading of the lumbar spine and flattening of the lordosis, which instantly switches off the defensive paraspinal muscle spasms and provides profound pain relief (negative reinforcement).
Action: Apply a hot pack to the lower back 20 minutes before attempting to stand. Consequence: Increased local blood flow softens the rigid, cramped muscle fibers, making the transition from sitting to standing significantly less jarring and painful (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Physiotherapy referral for core stabilization and lifting mechanics once acute spasms resolve.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days. Expect gradual improvement over 1-2 weeks; imaging is strictly not indicated unless red flags develop.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if there is sudden onset of bowel or bladder incontinence, numbness in the groin/saddle area, or new, severe shooting pain or weakness traveling down either leg.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a clinical assessment of mechanical joint/muscle strain. X-rays or MRI scans are not indicated for acute mechanical back pain without neurological signs. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute, locking spasms will generally unlock within 48-72 hours with muscle relaxants and heat. Total bed rest is harmful; gentle, frequent movement is required for healing.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call an ambulance immediately if you lose the ability to control your bladder or bowels, or if your legs suddenly become entirely numb or weak.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: You have strained a muscle in your lower back, causing the surrounding muscles to go into a massive, defensive lockdown (spasm) to protect the area. The injected anti-inflammatory reduces the pain, while the short course of Valium acts directly to force those cramped muscles to let go.
Actionable Steps: Do not stay strictly in bed. You must get up and walk short distances around the house every hour. Use the "calves on a chair" position to rest the back safely. Avoid bending or twisting for the next 3 days.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Acute lower back muscle spasm. Given muscle relaxants and anti-inflammatory injection. Avoid strict bed rest. Use heat packs and walk frequently. GP review in 3 days.

